# Stable name for the manual-override gamepad, and the start edge for the override.
#
# Matching on ID_INPUT_JOYSTICK rather than a vendor:product pair is deliberate. The pad
# is used over BOTH USB and Bluetooth, and the two present different product IDs -- and
# different axis layouts (USB: 8 axes, LT ax2 | BT: 6 axes, LT ax4). A VID:PID rule would
# have to enumerate every pad that might be used and would silently fail to start on any
# other. ID_INPUT_JOYSTICK is set by udev's builtin input_id for anything that presents
# joystick axes, over either transport. xbox_override resolves the axis map itself from
# the live /joy message, so the rule does not need to know which pad it matched.
#
# KERNEL=="event[0-9]*" restricts this to ONE match per pad. An input device presents
# several nodes (eventN always, jsN when joydev is loaded); without this the rule fires
# once per node and systemd is asked to start the unit repeatedly. The event node is the
# right one to bind to because it exists regardless of whether joydev is loaded.
#
# TAG+="systemd" makes systemd create a .device unit for the pad AND alias it to every
# DEVLINKS entry -- which is what gives dev-gamepad.device its name, the name
# ackermann-joystick.service binds to. Dropping this tag would leave the override running
# against a pad that is no longer there.
#
# SYSTEMD_WANTS is the start edge and it covers boot as well as a later hot-plug:
# systemd-udev-trigger.service replays add events for devices already present. The unit
# has no [Install] section, so this rule is the only thing that ever starts it.
SUBSYSTEM=="input", KERNEL=="event[0-9]*", ENV{ID_INPUT_JOYSTICK}=="1", \
    SYMLINK+="gamepad", TAG+="systemd", ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}="ackermann-joystick.service"
